# The ledger of `nats`: what a program does with a NATS server, measured
# against Go's `github.com/nats-io/nats.go` v1.56.0 and `nkeys` v0.4.17
# (their `go doc -all`, fetched into the scratch folder on 2026-11-18),
# Python's nats-py 2.17.0 (`dir(nats.aio.client.Client)`, the parameters of
# `connect`, `Msg`, `Subscription`, `nats.errors`, installed into the scratch
# folder the same day) and Rust's async-nats (its docs.rs pages read the same
# day: `Client`, `ConnectOptions`, the crate's items). JetStream, its
# key-value and object stores are the three libraries' largest part and stand
# in three blocks of their own, pending.
#
# The expected values of the tests were asked of Go: the NKey seed and public
# key of the bytes 0 to 31 and the signature of "abc" (`nkeys.FromRawSeed`,
# `Seed`, `PublicKey`, `Sign`), and how a header block is read
# (`nats.DecodeHeadersMsg`: a value's leading spaces taken, its trailing ones
# kept, a name compared as written, `Header.Get("foo-bar")` and
# `Get("Foo-Bar")` two names). The bytes of each operation are the protocol's
# own page's. The live run, on 2026-11-18, reached nats-server 2.10.27 of
# Debian 13 in a container of this Mac: plain, a token, a user and password
# with a publish permission denied, an NKey, TLS after INFO, TLS first
# (`handshake_first`) and a WebSocket.

capability nats/connect  a connection made to the first server of a list that answers, logged in, its PING answered
  heroes  nats.connect, nats.options, nats.parse_url, nats.Options, nats.Client, nats.DEFAULT_PORT
  go      Connect, Options, GetDefaultOptions, Options.Connect, Conn, DefaultURL, DefaultPort
  rust    connect, connect_with_options, ConnectOptions::new, ConnectOptions::connect, ServerAddr, ToServerAddrs, Client
  python  nats.connect, NATS, Client.connect (servers)
  note    the URL list is Go's and Python's comma-separated text; a server's URL that holds a user or a token logs in, as all three read it

capability nats/connect-timeout  the time a connection to one server may take
  heroes  nats.Options (connect_ms)
  go      Timeout, Dialer, SetCustomDialer
  rust    ConnectOptions::connection_timeout, ConnectOptions::local_address
  python  connect_timeout
  note    a custom dialer and a local address are refused below with the transports: `net` makes the connection

capability nats/pool  several servers, the servers a cluster tells of, tried in order or at random
  heroes  nats.Options (servers, discovered, randomize), nats.servers_of
  go      DontRandomize, IgnoreDiscoveredServers, Conn.Servers, Conn.DiscoveredServers, Conn.ServerPool, DiscoveredServersHandler, Conn.SetDiscoveredServersHandler
  rust    ConnectOptions::ignore_discovered_servers, ConnectOptions::retain_servers_order, Client::server_pool, ConnectOptions::verify_discovered_servers_by_ip
  python  dont_randomize, discovered_servers, servers, server_pool, discovered_server_cb
  note    the pool is tried in the options' order by default, where Go and Rust shuffle it: a program asks `randomize`; a discovered server is checked by its name, never by its address alone

capability nats/pool-changed  the pool replaced while connected
  heroes  pending: a connection made from new options serves meanwhile; the pool is read from the options and the servers' INFO alone
  go      Conn.SetServerPool, ReconnectToServer
  rust    Client::set_server_pool, ConnectOptions::reconnect_to_server_callback
  python  set_server_pool, reconnect_to_server_handler

capability nats/auth-token  a login by token
  heroes  nats.Auth (token)
  go      Token, TokenHandler
  rust    ConnectOptions::with_token, ConnectOptions::token
  python  token
  note    a token computed at each connection by a function (Go's TokenHandler) is the program's own: it builds the options anew

capability nats/auth-user  a login by user and password
  heroes  nats.Auth (user)
  go      UserInfo
  rust    ConnectOptions::with_user_and_password, ConnectOptions::user_and_password
  python  user, password

capability nats/auth-nkey  a login by an NKey: the server's nonce signed with Ed25519
  heroes  nats.Auth (nkey)
  go      Nkey, NkeyOptionFromSeed
  rust    ConnectOptions::with_nkey, ConnectOptions::nkey
  python  nkeys_seed, nkeys_seed_str, signature_cb
  note    the signature is the system's (`crypto/keypairs`): Linux has Ed25519, macOS and Windows do not, and there it fails `unsupported`; a signature made by a function of the program (Python's signature_cb) is refused below

capability nats/auth-jwt  a login by a user's JWT and its seed, as a .creds file holds them
  heroes  nats.Auth (credentials), nats.parse_credentials, nats.Credentials
  go      UserCredentials, UserCredentialBytes, UserJWTAndSeed, UserJWT
  rust    ConnectOptions::with_credentials, ConnectOptions::with_credentials_file, ConnectOptions::credentials, ConnectOptions::credentials_file, ConnectOptions::jwt, ConnectOptions::with_jwt
  python  user_credentials, user_jwt_cb
  note    the file is read by the program (`fs` or `read_file`) and given as text; Go's UserJWT with a signing callback is the seed given directly

capability nats/auth-callback  a login computed by a function of the program at each connection
  heroes  refused: the login is a value of the options; a function asked at each connection would carry the program's state, which a function value cannot hold (no closure, README B3)
  go      -
  rust    ConnectOptions::with_auth_callback, Auth, AuthError
  python  signature_cb, user_jwt_cb

capability nats/nkeys  NKeys read and written: a seed's public key, a signature, a check, a new user's seed
  heroes  nats.nkey_public_of, nats.nkey_sign, nats.nkey_verify, nats.generate_user_seed
  go      nkeys.FromSeed, nkeys.FromRawSeed, nkeys.FromPublicKey, nkeys.CreateUser, nkeys.KeyPair, nkeys.Decode, nkeys.Encode, nkeys.EncodeSeed, nkeys.DecodeSeed, nkeys.IsValidPublicUserKey, nkeys.ParseDecoratedJWT, nkeys.ParseDecoratedNKey, nkeys.ParseDecoratedUserNKey, nkeys.PrefixByte
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    users' keys alone are made here; an account's, an operator's and a server's are read (their seeds sign), and curve keys (X25519, `X`) are refused, NATS using them for xkeys, not for a login

capability nats/tls  TLS after the server's INFO, the certificate checked against the system's store or the program's authorities
  heroes  nats.Options (tls, roots), servers `tls://`
  go      Secure, RootCAs, ClientTLSConfig, Conn.TLSRequired, Conn.TLSConnectionState
  rust    ConnectOptions::require_tls, ConnectOptions::add_root_certificates, ConnectOptions::tls_client_config, ConnectOptions::tls_server_name, ConnectOptions::tls_optional
  python  tls, tls_hostname
  note    a server that asks for TLS gets it whatever the options say, as all three do; on macOS `tls` begins TLS only over a connection it makes, so the client connects again past the INFO line there (`tls.connect_after`), the server's limits read from the first connection's INFO

capability nats/tls-first  TLS from the first byte, for a server configured with handshake_first
  heroes  nats.Options (tls_first)
  go      TLSHandshakeFirst
  rust    ConnectOptions::tls_first
  python  tls_handshake_first

capability nats/tls-client-certificate  a certificate of the client's own (mutual TLS)
  heroes  pending: `tls.Client` holds no certificate of the client's own to present
  go      ClientCert
  rust    ConnectOptions::add_client_certificate
  python  tls (an ssl.SSLContext with a certificate)

capability nats/websocket  NATS over a WebSocket, ws:// and wss://
  heroes  nats.Options (servers `ws://`, `wss://`)
  go      ProxyPath, Compression
  rust    -
  python  servers ws://
  note    the opening request's own headers and its compression are pending in the block below

capability nats/websocket-options  the WebSocket's opening headers and its compression
  heroes  pending: `websocket.Client` takes both, and the options do not carry them yet
  go      WebSocketConnectionHeaders, WebSocketConnectionHeadersHandler, Compression
  rust    -
  python  ws_connection_headers

capability nats/publish  a message published to a subject
  heroes  nats.publish, nats.message
  go      Conn.Publish
  rust    Client::publish
  python  publish
  note    answered once the bytes are written, as Go and Python answer; `flush` waits for the server

capability nats/publish-reply  a message published with a reply subject, its headers and its data
  heroes  nats.publish_message, nats.Message
  go      Conn.PublishRequest, Conn.PublishMsg, NewMsg, Msg
  rust    Client::publish_with_reply, Client::publish_with_reply_and_headers, message::Message, message::OutboundMessage
  python  publish (reply), Msg

capability nats/headers  headers written with HPUB and read from HMSG, the server's status among them
  heroes  nats.Header, nats.header_of, nats.headers_of, nats.with_header, nats.Message (headers, status, description)
  go      Header, Header.Add, Header.Get, Header.Set, Header.Del, Header.Values, DecodeHeadersMsg, Conn.HeadersSupported, MsgHeaders
  rust    Client::publish_with_headers, header::HeaderMap, header::HeaderName, header::HeaderValue, header::GetAll, status::StatusCode
  python  publish (headers), Msg.headers, Msg.header
  note    a name is compared as written, as Go's Header.Get answered on 2026-11-18; Go's Header.Set and Del are a list's own operations on `Message.headers`

capability nats/subscribe  a subscription, its messages read in order, many subscriptions on one connection
  heroes  nats.subscribe, nats.next_message, nats.next_message_of, nats.subscriptions_of
  go      Conn.Subscribe, Conn.SubscribeSync, Conn.ChanSubscribe, Subscription, Subscription.NextMsg, Subscription.NextMsgWithContext, Subscription.Msgs, Subscription.MsgsTimeout, Conn.NumSubscriptions, MsgHandler
  rust    Client::subscribe, Subscriber
  python  subscribe, Subscription.next_msg, Subscription.messages, Subscription.subject
  note    a message is read by the program, never handed to a callback (no closure); a subscription is its number, which its messages carry

capability nats/queue  a queue group: each message to one member
  heroes  nats.queue_subscribe
  go      Conn.QueueSubscribe, Conn.QueueSubscribeSync, Conn.ChanQueueSubscribe, Conn.QueueSubscribeSyncWithChan
  rust    Client::queue_subscribe
  python  subscribe (queue), Subscription.queue

capability nats/unsubscribe  a subscription ended, at once or after a count
  heroes  nats.unsubscribe, nats.unsubscribe_after
  go      Subscription.Unsubscribe, Subscription.AutoUnsubscribe, Subscription.IsValid
  rust    Subscriber::unsubscribe, Subscriber::unsubscribe_after
  python  Subscription.unsubscribe (limit)

capability nats/pending  the messages held unread bounded, the dropped ones counted
  heroes  nats.pending_of, nats.dropped_of, nats.delivered_of, nats.Options (pending_messages, pending_bytes)
  go      Subscription.Pending, Subscription.Dropped, Subscription.Delivered, Subscription.SetPendingLimits, Subscription.PendingLimits, Subscription.MaxPending, Subscription.ClearMaxPending, Subscription.QueuedMsgs, DefaultSubPendingMsgsLimit, DefaultSubPendingBytesLimit, SyncQueueLen
  rust    ConnectOptions::subscription_capacity, ConnectOptions::client_capacity
  python  Subscription.pending_msgs, Subscription.pending_bytes, Subscription.delivered, pending_size
  note    the bound is the client's, every subscription together, where Go's is each subscription's; a message past it is dropped and counted on its subscription, as Go's slow consumer is

capability nats/request  a request published and its first reply read, through one inbox subscription of the client's
  heroes  nats.request, nats.request_message, nats.new_inbox
  go      Conn.Request, Conn.RequestMsg, Conn.RequestWithContext, Conn.RequestMsgWithContext, NewInbox, Conn.NewInbox, Conn.NewRespInbox, CustomInboxPrefix, InboxPrefix
  rust    Client::request, Client::request_with_headers, Client::send_request, Client::new_inbox, client::Request, ConnectOptions::custom_inbox_prefix, ConnectOptions::request_timeout, Client::timeout
  python  request, new_inbox, inbox_prefix

capability nats/respond  a reply published to a request's reply subject
  heroes  nats.respond
  go      Msg.Respond, Msg.RespondMsg
  rust    -
  python  Msg.respond

capability nats/no-responders  a request no one listens to told at once, by the server's status 503
  heroes  nats.request
  go      ErrNoResponders
  rust    client::RequestErrorKind (NoResponders)
  python  errors.NoRespondersError
  note    it fails `not_found`; a server that reads no headers answers nothing, and the request times out

capability nats/old-request  one subscription made and ended per request
  heroes  refused: the inbox subscription the client keeps answers every request; Go keeps this form for servers before 2.2 alone
  go      UseOldRequestStyle
  rust    -
  python  -

capability nats/flush  every byte written read by the server, by a PING and its PONG; the time of a round trip
  heroes  nats.flush, nats.rtt
  go      Conn.Flush, Conn.FlushTimeout, Conn.FlushWithContext, Conn.RTT, FlusherTimeout
  rust    Client::flush
  python  flush, rtt, flush_timeout

capability nats/drain  subscriptions ended, the messages already sent kept and read, the connection closed
  heroes  nats.drain
  go      Conn.Drain, Subscription.Drain, Conn.IsDraining, Subscription.IsDraining, DrainTimeout
  rust    Client::drain, client::DrainError
  python  drain, drain_timeout, is_draining, is_draining_pubs
  note    one subscription drained alone (Go's Subscription.Drain) is `unsubscribe` after reading what is held

capability nats/close  the connection closed
  heroes  nats.close, nats.is_closed
  go      Conn.Close, Conn.IsClosed, Conn.IsConnected
  rust    -
  python  close, is_closed, is_connected

capability nats/reconnect  a lost connection made again with a backoff, the subscriptions sent anew, a write sent again
  heroes  nats.Options (reconnect), nats.Reconnect, nats.default_reconnect, nats.reconnect, nats.stats_of (reconnects)
  go      MaxReconnects, ReconnectWait, ReconnectJitter, CustomReconnectDelay, NoReconnect, ReconnectBufSize, Conn.ForceReconnect, Conn.IsReconnecting, ReconnectOnFlusherError, DefaultMaxReconnect, DefaultReconnectWait
  rust    ConnectOptions::max_reconnects, ConnectOptions::reconnect_delay_callback, Client::force_reconnect, client::ReconnectError
  python  allow_reconnect, reconnect_time_wait, max_reconnect_attempts, force_reconnect, is_reconnecting
  note    the backoff doubles from `wait_ms` to `most_wait_ms` with a random part, as Rust's does, where Go waits the same each time; the reconnection runs inside the call that found the loss, so nothing is buffered meanwhile, and the write that failed is sent once more

capability nats/retry-first-connect  a connection answered before any server is reached, made in the background
  heroes  refused: a client that answers before it is connected needs a loop running beside the program; the program calls `connect` again
  go      RetryOnFailedConnect
  rust    ConnectOptions::retry_on_initial_connect
  python  -

capability nats/ping  the client's own PINGs, a connection taken as lost after so many go unanswered
  heroes  nats.Options (ping_interval_ms, pings_outstanding)
  go      PingInterval, MaxPingsOutstanding
  rust    ConnectOptions::ping_interval
  python  ping_interval, max_outstanding_pings
  note    a PING is sent while a call waits, the program's own loop being the only one

capability nats/info  what the server tells of itself: its version, its limits, the client's identity
  heroes  nats.server_info_of, nats.ServerInfo, nats.max_payload_of, nats.connected_url_of
